theory.intro:
The **distance formula** gives the length of the interval joining two points from their coordinates. It is Pythagoras' theorem applied to the right-angled triangle whose shorter sides are the run and the rise: $d = \sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\draw[<->] (-0.4,0)--(6.6,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.4)--(0,4.6) node[above]{$y$};
\coordinate (A) at (1.6,1.0);
\coordinate (B) at (5.0,3.6);
\coordinate (C) at (5.0,1.0);
\draw[dashed,gray] (A)--(C)--(B);
\draw[thick,blue] (A)--(B);
\draw ($(C)+(-0.35,0)$)--($(C)+(-0.35,0.35)$)--($(C)+(0,0.35)$);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below] at (A) {$A(x_1,y_1)$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above] at (B) {$B(x_2,y_2)$};
\node[below] at (3.9,1.0) {$x_2-x_1$};
\node[right] at (5.0,2.3) {$y_2-y_1$};
\node at (2.8,2.6) {$d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A support cable runs from $A(-2, 1)$ to $B(1, 5)$.
Find the length of the interval $AB$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->] (-5.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-5.5)--(0,5.5) node[above]{$y$};
\foreach \i in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\i,0){$\i$};
\foreach \i in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,\i){$\i$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-2,1)--(1,5);
\fill (-2,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\scriptsize] at (-2,1){$A(-2,1)$};
\fill (1,5) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\scriptsize] at (1,5){$B(1,5)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
$d = \sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}$
$= \sqrt{(1 - (-2))^2 + (5 - 1)^2}$
$= \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}$
$= \sqrt{9 + 16}$
$= \sqrt{25}$
$= 5$
